{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tor functor tor functor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md","fileName":"Tor Functor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tor Functor","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 20:20\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Homological Algebra and Derived Functors]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Homological Algebra and Derived Functors","Projective Resolution","Flat Module","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tor functor tor functor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md","fileName":"Tor Functor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tor Functor","heading":"Tor Functor","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Torᵢᴿ(M,N) is the i-th homology of P• ⊗_R N, where P• is a [[Projective Resolution]] of M. The resulting module is independent of the chosen resolution up to isomorphism.\n\nTor is symmetric in M and N over a commutative ring. Positive-degree Tor measures the failure of tensor product to preserve exactness and vanishes for [[Flat Module]]s.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Homological Algebra and Derived Functors","Projective Resolution","Flat Module","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tor functor tor functor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tor Functor.md","fileName":"Tor Functor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tor Functor","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[commutat